=== 1789573187919-fn27l8#1 (gpt-6-astra)
|Your truth value and reasoning are correct! Taking x = 5 gives
|  (x − 4)(y + 2) = y + 2
|for every real y.
|
|Your negation has the quantifiers right, but you also need to change =
+to ≠:
|
|Negation: For every x ∈ ℝ, there exists y ∈ ℝ such that
|  xy + 2x − 4y − 8 ≠ y + 2.
|
|Original statement: True, with x = 5.
=== 1789573187919-fn27l8#3 (gpt-6-astra)
|Yes—your negation is correct, and the original statement is
+true.
|
|For any x > 0, choose a natural number
|      1
+  n > ─.
+      x
|Then 0 < 1/n < x. Natural numbers are unbounded, so such an n always
+exists.
|
|Your proposed n = 1/x has two issues: it might not be a natural number,
+and it gives 1/n = x, whereas you need strictly less.
|
|About 0.000… “eventually followed by a 1”:
|- If the 1 occurs at a finite decimal position, the number is positive,
+and a sufficiently large n works.
|- If every decimal digit is 0, the number is just 0, which is excluded
+by x > 0. There’s no position “after infinitely many zeros.”
|
|That’s different from incomputability; even for an incomputable positive
+real, the statement holds.
=== 1789573187919-fn27l8#5 (gpt-6-astra)
|Correct! Just make the proof slightly clearer by establishing x = 1
+before concluding x ≤ a:
|
|My set: A = {1}.
|
|Proof. Take a = 2. For any x ∈ A, we have x = 1. Since 1 ≤ 2, we have
+x ≤ a. Thus, A is excited.
|
|Follow-up: a is not unique (you wrote uppercase A): any a ≥ 1 works.
+Yes, the allowable choices of a depend on A, because a must be at least
+as large as every element of A.
=== 1789573187919-fn27l8#7 (gpt-6-astra)
|Yes, correct! A slightly cleaner proof:
|
|Take a = 1 ∈ A. For any x ∈ A = {1}, we have x = 1, so x ≤ a. Thus,
+A is happy.
|
|And yes, a is unique—in fact, this holds for every happy set: if
+a, b both satisfy the definition, then a ≤ b and b ≤ a, so a = b.
|
|For the hint: a does not depend on x; one fixed a must work for
+every x ∈ A.
=== 1789573187919-fn27l8#9 (gpt-6-astra)
|Exactly—a depends on the set A, but not on the individual x ∈ A.
+Those are compatible statements.
|
|You choose one a for the whole set, and that same a must work for every
+element x.
