|The paper does not explicitly report training FLOPs for
+LeWorldModel. I checked the PDF/arXiv text: it gives architecture, batch
+size, epochs, datasets, and hardware, but no “we trained for X FLOPs”
+number.
|
|What it does state:
|
|- LeWM has ~15M parameters
|- Encoder: ViT-Tiny, patch size 14, 12 layers, hidden dim 192, ~5M
+params
|- Predictor: transformer, 6 layers, ~10M params
|- Training: batch size 128
|- Sequence/subtrajectory: 4 frames, each 224×224
|- Frame skip: 5
|- Trained for 10 epochs
|- Hardware: single NVIDIA L40S GPU
|- Abstract says trainable “in a few hours”
|
|Your local GPU appears to be a Sapphire Radeon RX 5700 XT 8GB.
|
|## My best FLOP estimate
|
|Using the architecture and dataset details, a reasonable
+training-compute estimate is:
|
|### Per 4-frame training sample
|
|A ViT-Tiny forward pass at 224×224 with 14×14 patches is roughly 3.4
+GFLOPs per image. Training forward+backward is about 3× forward,
+and each sample has 4 frames:
|
|  3.4 GFLOPs × 4 × 3 ≈ 41 GFLOPs/sample
|
|The predictor is much smaller in compute because it operates on only a
+few latent tokens, so I’d round this to about:
|
|  ┌──────────────────────────────────┐
+  │ 40–45 GFLOPs per training sample │
+  └──────────────────────────────────┘
|
|### PushT training run
|
|PushT dataset in the paper:
|
|- 20,000 episodes
|- Average length 196 env steps
|- Frame skip 5 → about 39 latent frames/episode
|- 4-frame windows → about 36 usable windows/episode
|- 10 epochs
|
|Approximate samples:
|
|  20,000 × 36 × 10 ≈ 7.2M samples
|
|Total training FLOPs:
|
|  7.2M × 41 GFLOPs ≈ 3.0 × 10¹⁷ FLOPs
|
|So for one LeWM run on PushT:
|
|  ┌───────────────────────────────┐
+  │ ≈ 3 × 10¹⁷ FLOPs ≈ 0.3 EFLOPs │
+  └───────────────────────────────┘
|
|Across the four main environments in the paper, one training run each is
+roughly:
|
|  ┌──────────────────┐
+  │ ≈ 0.6–0.7 EFLOPs │
+  └──────────────────┘
|
|## Time on your RX 5700 XT
|
|Your RX 5700 XT has roughly:
|
|- FP32 peak: 9.75 TFLOPs
|- FP16 peak: 19.5 TFLOPs
|- VRAM: 8GB
|
|For the estimated 0.3 EFLOPs PushT run:
|
|### Theoretical best case
|
|At FP16 peak:
|
|  3 × 10¹⁷/19.5 × 10¹² ≈ 15,400 s ≈ 4.3 hours
|
|At FP32 peak:
|
|  3 × 10¹⁷/9.75 × 10¹² ≈ 30,800 s ≈ 8.6 hours
|
|So, absolute theoretical lower bound:
|
|  ┌─────────────────────────────────────┐
+  │ ≈ 4–9 hours for one PushT-scale run │
+  └─────────────────────────────────────┘
|
|### More realistic
|
|Consumer AMD GPU training will not hit peak. Also, RX 5700 XT / RDNA1
+has weak/awkward ML software support compared with an NVIDIA L40S. If
+you get 20–35% effective utilization, then:
|
|  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────┐
+  │ ≈ 12–40 hours for one PushT-scale LeWM run │
+  └────────────────────────────────────────────┘
|
|For all four environments:
|
|  ┌────────────────────────────┐
+  │ ≈ 1.5–4 days realistically │
+  └────────────────────────────┘
|
|Possibly longer if ROCm/PyTorch support gives you trouble or if batch
+size 128 does not fit in 8GB VRAM.
|
|## Alternative interpretation: matching their L40S wall-clock compute
|
|If instead you interpret “how many FLOPs they used” as “L40S peak FLOPs
+× a few hours,” then the number is much larger. NVIDIA L40S peak FP16
+tensor throughput is about 733 TFLOPs. If “a few hours” means 3–5
+hours:
|
|  733 × 10¹² × 3–5 h ≈ 7.9–13.2 EFLOPs
|
|Your RX 5700 XT at 19.5 TFLOPs FP16 would need:
|
|  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────┐
+  │ ≈ 4.7–7.8 days at theoretical FP16 peak │
+  └─────────────────────────────────────────┘
|
|Realistically, perhaps:
|
|  ┌───────────────┐
+  │ ≈ 1–3 + weeks │
+  └───────────────┘
|
|But I think this is a less meaningful estimate because the L40S almost
+certainly was not running at FP16 tensor-core peak for this small
+15M-parameter ViT-style model.
|
|## Bottom line
|
|Best estimate from the paper’s actual training setup:
|
|  One LeWorldModel training run:  ∼ 0.1–0.3 EFLOPs depending on dataset
|
|For the main PushT setup:
|
|  ┌──────────────┐
+  │ ∼ 0.3 EFLOPs │
+  └──────────────┘
|
|On your RX 5700 XT:
|
|  ┌────────────────────────┐
+  │ Theoretical: 4–9 hours │
+  └────────────────────────┘
|
|  ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────┐
+  │ Realistic: 12–40 hours for one comparable run │
+  └───────────────────────────────────────────────┘
|
|If you wanted to reproduce all main environment runs, expect more like:
|
|  ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
