=== 1779056231816-wg384n#53 (gpt-5.5)
|Yes, I agree with the UX direction.
|
|Conceptually, a context summary is assistant-authored compressed
+memory, not a real user turn. So the current persisted-history shape:
|
|▎ text
|▎ user: [Summary of turns A–B]
|▎ assistant: <summary>
|
|is clunky. It was probably done because it gives the model an explicit
+header in the provider transcript, but UX-wise it is weird and creates a
+fake user message.
|
|I’d prefer a unified summary representation:
|
|▎ text
|▎ assistant:
|▎   metadata.kind = "context_summary"
|▎   metadata.summaryOf = { start, end, scope? }
|▎   content = <summary text>
|
|Rendered as an AI message with a header:
|
|▎ text
|▎ Summary of turns 123–180
|▎ <summary text>
|
|For current/in-progress summaries:
|
|▎ text
|▎ Summary of in-progress turns 900–912
|▎ <summary text>
|
|or maybe same wording if we don’t care to distinguish:
|
|▎ text
|▎ Summary of turns 900–912
|▎ <summary text>
|
|## Why this is better
|
|- No fake user message.
|- Persisted-history and in-progress summaries render the same way.
|- The model still sees the summary as assistant-authored content.
|- The UI can style it as an AI summary block, not dim system text.
|- It avoids the weird [Summary of turns] / assistant-pair split.
|- It reduces message count: one replacement turn instead of two for
+persisted summaries.
|
|## One caveat
|
|We need the header to remain model-visible somehow.
|
|If we only make the header a UI-only field and send just the summary
+text to the model, the model loses the boundary information. So I’d
+either:
|
|1. keep the header in the assistant text content:
|
|▎ text
|▎ [Summary of turns 123–180]
|▎ <summary>
|
|and have TUI render that header specially, or
|
|2. add metadata and make provider serialization include a prefix.
|
|Option 1 is simpler and safer.
|
|## Implementation shape I’d choose
|
|For both persisted and current summarize replacements, create a single
+assistant message:
|
|▎ ts
|▎ {
|▎   role: "assistant",
|▎   content: \`[Summary of turns \${start}–\${end}]\\n\${summaryText}\`,
|▎   metadata: {
|▎     system: true,
|▎     kind: "context_summary",
|▎     ...
|▎   }
|▎ }
|
|Actually, I would not set system: true if that makes the TUI render it
+as a system notice. Instead, we may want something like:
|
|▎ ts
|▎ metadata: {
|▎   kind: "context_summary",
|▎   summaryOf: { start, end, scope }
|▎ }
|
|But MessageMetadata currently has only loose kind?: string, so adding
+typed optional metadata fields may be worth it.
|
|Alternatively, keep metadata normal/null and rely on the text prefix:
|
|▎ text
|▎ [Summary of turns A–B]
|▎ ...
|
|Then update display rendering to detect this prefix inside assistant
+text and render it with a summary header. That avoids changing schema.
|
|## My recommendation
|
|Do this:
|
|▎ text
|▎ assistant message only
|▎ content:
|▎   [Summary of turns A–B]
|▎   <summary text>
|▎ metadata.kind = "context_summary"
|
|For in-progress:
|
|▎ text
|▎ assistant message only
|▎ content:
|▎   [Summary of in-progress turns A–B]
|▎   <summary text>
|▎ metadata.kind = "context_summary"
|
|Then the TUI renders any assistant text beginning with that summary
+prefix as:
|
|▎ text
|▎ Summary of turns A–B
|▎ <summary>
|
|with AI-message styling, not dim system styling.
|
|So yes: one AI summary message for both persisted and in-progress is
+cleaner, more truthful, and better UX.
