=== 1773526116281-9b6wy7#3 (opus)
|Honestly, it was a mixed bag. Here's the breakdown:
|
|## What worked well
|- Parallelizing independent file creation — I dispatched subagents
+for highlight.ts, formatting.ts, codeblocks.ts + tables.ts, and
+wordwrap.ts + index.ts without blocking on each other. That saved real
+time since each file was self-contained.
|- The agents followed instructions well — They created the files
+with the right imports, the right ANSI constants, and mostly-correct
+implementations. The Mnemo regex rules were copied faithfully.
|
|## The hiccoughs
|
|1. Quality wasn't perfect on first pass. The tables.ts subagent
+introduced a type error — it tried to pass formatMarkdown() (which
+returns {text, plain}) directly to wrapCell() (which expects string).
+I had to rewrite the entire renderTableBlock function myself to match
+Mnemo's proper approach (wrap on raw markdown text, format per-cell
+during assembly). That was the biggest fix.
|
|2. The code review subagent timed out. I dispatched a haiku agent to
+do a thorough code quality review and it hit the 120s timeout without
+producing output. I ended up doing the review myself, which was probably
+better anyway since I had all the code in context already.
|
|3. isFenceClose was wrong. The codeblocks subagent wrote a naive
+version that trimmed the whole line and checked for backticks — which
+would match  \`\`\`some text\`\`\`  as a closing fence. I had to fix it to
+use Mnemo's proper regex (/^ {0,3}({3,})\\s*$/\`).
|
|4. The ANSI stripping regex was sloppy. One agent used
+/\\x1b\\[[^m]*m/g instead of /\\x1b\\[[0-9;]*m/g — the [^m]* variant is
+greedy in weird ways and could match across escape sequences. Small but
+important fix.
|
|## Takeaways
|
|- Subagents are great for boilerplate-heavy, spec-driven work (like
+porting 500 lines of regex rules). They saved me from having to manually
+copy all 14 language definitions.
|- They're less reliable for architectural decisions — the tables.ts
+agent didn't understand how the formatting pipeline should flow (wrap
+first, format during assembly). That required domain understanding of
+how Mnemo does it.
|- The orchestrator still needs to verify everything. I wouldn't
+trust subagent output without a compile check + visual test. The
+build→verify loop caught real bugs.
|- For code reviews, I should've just done it myself from the start
+instead of waiting 2+ minutes for a timeout.
|
|If I were doing it again, I'd use subagents for the leaf modules
+(highlight.ts, formatting.ts) and write the integration-heavy pieces
+(tables.ts, wordwrap.ts, conversation.ts changes) myself from the start.
=== 1773090429370-m6qmfm#1 (opus)
|Done. Here's a summary of the changes:
|
|Problem: In the chat history panel, $ and 0 operated on individual
+visual (word-wrapped) lines instead of full logical (\\n-delimited)
+lines. A long paragraph wrapped across 3 visual lines would have $ stop
+at the edge of the first visual line instead of jumping to the end of
+the paragraph.
|
|Root cause: buildMessageLines() pre-wraps text via wordWrap() into a
+flat array of visual lines. The history motions lineStart/lineEnd in
+historymotions.ts treated each array element as a line, with no
+knowledge of which visual lines belonged to the same logical line.
|
|Fix across 5 files:
|
|┌───────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
|│ File  │ Change                                                       │
