|## Findings
|
|1. High — common currency ranges are
+misparsed as math and lose dollar signs. 
|findDollarClose pairs the next $, while
+likelyDollarMath accepts almost any
+non-numeric content containing an
+alphanumeric character. Thus:
|- Range: $5-$10. → Range: 5-10.
|- Basic ($5) / Pro ($10) → Basic (5) /
+Pro (10)
|This changes both displayed and copied
+meaning. The existing tests only cover
+amounts separated by whitespace, which
+happen to evade the heuristic. 
|Refs: tui/src/markdown/math.ts:388-408,
+tui/src/markdown/math.ts:441-453,
+tui/src/markdown/math.test.ts:64-69.
|
|2. Medium — hard newlines are hidden from
+the dollar parser, enabling invalid
+multiline spans and currency corruption. 
|likelyDollarMath explicitly rejects \\n, but
+renderInlineMathChunks replaces physical
+newlines with a private-use character before
+scanning. Consequently:
|- ["$5", "$10"] → ["5", "10"]
|- ["The variable is $x", "and then y$."]
+is incorrectly rendered as one math span.
|This contradicts the parser’s intended
+single-line-dollar rule. 
|Refs: tui/src/markdown/math.ts:388-393,
+tui/src/markdown/math.ts:460-480.
|
|3. Medium — display math immediately
+following prose is not recognized as a
+display block. 
|Display blocks are checked only at the outer
+loop’s current line; the paragraph collector
+does not stop when it encounters a display
+opener. For:
|▎ text
|▎ intro
|▎ \\[
|▎ \\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\\\c&d\\end{pmatrix}
|▎ \\]
|the output contains blank delimiter rows and
+compact inline (a b; c d) instead of the
+structural two-row matrix and copy
+projections. A blank line before \\[ makes it
+work, but LaTeX display delimiters should
+not depend on that. 
|Refs: tui/src/markdown/wordwrap.ts:106-118,
+tui/src/markdown/wordwrap.ts:143-158.
|
|4. Medium — malformed delimiter input has
+quadratic scanning behavior and can freeze
+the synchronous TUI renderer. 
|Every unclosed \\( invokes a scan to the end,
+then the outer scanner advances only one
+character. A 100,000-character string of
+repeated unclosed \\( took about 4.1
+seconds locally; cost grows quadratically.
+Streaming or resizing can trigger repeated
+renders. 
|Refs: tui/src/markdown/math.ts:359-365,
+tui/src/markdown/math.ts:430-439.
|
|5. Medium — raw delimiter splitting breaks
+valid mathematical content in tables and
+environments.
|- Table cells are split on every literal |
+before math parsing, so | $x|y$ | becomes
+two cells and the math remains unrendered.
|- Matrices split on every & before escaped
+ampersands are protected, so \\text{A \\& B}
+is treated as multiple entries. Display
+matrices also do not compute/pad column
+widths.
|Refs: tui/src/markdown/tables.ts:37-43,
+tui/src/markdown/math.ts:278-309,
+tui/src/markdown/math.ts:322-327.
|
|6. Medium — malformed and unsupported TeX
+is silently rewritten rather than safely
+preserved. 
|The try/catch only helps if the dependency
+throws. With latexCheck: false, it
+tolerates malformed input and strips
+grouping:
|- \\(\\frac{{{{\\) → \\frac
|- \\(x+\\unknown{y}\\) → x+\\unknowny
|The test named “preserves unknown commands
+and survives malformed input” merely checks
+that a substring remains and that no
+exception occurs; it does not check
+preservation or readable fallback. 
|Refs: tui/src/markdown/math.ts:313-350,
+tui/src/markdown/math.test.ts:52-55.
|
|7. Low — display wrapping can split a wide
+supplementary code point into invalid UTF-16
+halves at width 1. 
|If sliceByWidth cannot fit a width-2
+grapheme, the fallback uses rest[0]. For
+renderDisplayMath("😀x", 1), the emoji
+becomes two lone-surrogate rows.
+Narrow/wide/combining copy behavior is
+untested. 
|Refs: tui/src/markdown/math.ts:521-533.
|
|## Dependency risk
|
|tui/package.json:13 adds exact-pinned
+@devhub-io/latex-to-unicode@2.0.1.
+Positives: it is small, MIT-licensed, has no
+runtime dependencies or install scripts, and
+the version is exact. However, it was first
+published only six days ago, has one
+maintainer, and the repository tracks no
+lockfile/integrity record. That is a
+meaningful maintenance/supply-chain risk for
+a core rendering path.
|
|## Strengths
|
|- Correctly protects supported fenced and
+inline code spans, including multiline
+inline-code spans.
|- Limits rendering to assistant markdown
+mode.
|- Uses terminal-column-aware width helpers
+and provides copy projections for display
+math.
|- Covers common logic symbols, scripts,
+roots, fractions, environments, code
+protection, tables, and basic currency
+examples.
|- Conversion exceptions are contained rather
+than crashing the conversation.
|
|## Validation and readiness
|
|- bun test: 635 passed, 0 failed.
|- Math/word-wrap subset: 23 passed, 0
+failed.
|- git diff --check: clean.
|- Typecheck fails on four
+historycursor.test.ts errors, but the same
+failures occur on local main, so they are
+not introduced by this branch.
|
|Not ready to merge. The currency
+corruption is a merge blocker. The
+multiline-dollar, display-block boundary,
+and quadratic malformed-input cases should
+also be fixed and covered by regression
+tests before merging.
=== 1784566665055-zjr3js#5 (gpt-5.6-sol)
|## Findings
|
|### 1. High — system-instructions offset can
+oscillate, leaving panel geometry
+inconsistent with the composed viewport
|
|Locations: render.ts:802-842,
+render.ts:1283-1320
|
|The bounded compositor interprets
+panelTopOffset as a canonical prefix
+beginning at viewStart:
|
|▎ ts
|▎ const instructions = canonicalViewportRows
+▎ (
|▎   allLines,
|▎   viewStart,
|▎   viewStart + instructionRows,
|▎ );
|
|But the outer loop recalculates the offset
+from the first row after top clipping:
|
|▎ ts
|▎ const topLineIndex = viewportRows[0]?.line
+▎ Index ?? viewStart;
|▎ const nextOffset =
|▎   visibleSystemInstructionsHeight(lineAnch
+▎ ors, topLineIndex, ...);
|
|If clipping removes ordinary history and
+exposes a later system-instructions box, the
+next attempt sets panelTopOffset to that
+box’s height. The compositor then
+incorrectly treats the first P canonical
+rows at the original viewStart as
+instructions. This can alternate between P
+= 0 and P = instruction height.
|
|Because the outer loop ends after three
+attempts even when the last attempt changes
+the offset, viewportRows can have been
+composed with one offset while
+taskPanelTopOffset and taskPanelRect use
+another.
|
|I reproduced this with:
|
|- cols = 120, rows = 30
|- one assistant row
|- system_instructions: "SYSTEM BOX HERE"
|- thirteen subsequent short assistant rows
|- 27 tasks
|
|The resulting state had:
|
|- taskPanelRect.top === 6, implying a
+three-row instructions prefix
|- the first three viewport rows all mapping
+to the same system_instructions_top
+canonical line, meaning that border was
+actually narrow-wrapped
|- the preceding assistant row clipped
|
|Thus the instruction box was not full-width
+even though the panel was positioned below
+it. This also causes repeated recomposition
+on subsequent uncached renders.
|
|The bounded layout should resolve the actual
+source start and instructions prefix
+together, or eliminate the outer feedback
+loop. At minimum, it must not update the
+panel offset after the final allowed attempt
+without recomposing with that offset.
|
|────────────────────────────────────────
|
|### 2. High for the performance objective —
+composition is not actually O(viewport
+height) for long user messages
|
|Locations: render.ts:592-645, called twice
+at render.ts:820-839
|
|For every user group touched by the narrow
+probe, composeFixedWidthRows:
|
|1. Scans backward to the beginning of the
+complete owner group.
|2. Scans forward to its complete end.
|3. Runs userMessageTextRowOffsets over the
+complete user text.
|4. Runs the same work again during suffix
+recomposition.
|
|Therefore, if a user message contains M
+canonical rows but only 13 touch the
+viewport, cold bounded composition remains
+Θ(M), not Θ(H). userMessageTextRowOffsets
+also allocates offsets for the complete
+message.
|
|The change to a monotone canonicalLocal
+pointer correctly removes one previous
+quadratic mapping cost, but group discovery
+and full-text flow indexing remain unbounded
+by viewport height.
|
|A user-flow index needs to be cached by
+owner/canonical width, or canonical anchors
+need to carry their UserMessageFlowCursor so
+the viewport can begin at an arbitrary
+canonical row without rescanning the entire
+owner. Without that, this implementation
+removes the fixed-point multiplier but does
+not meet the stated complexity guarantee.
|
|────────────────────────────────────────
|
|### 3. Medium — the full-width portion of a
+user bubble bypasses suffix recomposition
+and can have incorrect bubble geometry
|
|Location: render.ts:841
|
|The narrow suffix is recomposed adaptively,
+but the full tail is copied directly:
|
|▎ ts
|▎ const fullRows =
|▎   canonicalViewportRows(allLines, fullStar
+▎ tIndex, endLineIndex);
|
|Canonical user rows were styled by
+renderUserMessage using the maximum content
+width of the whole message. The previous
+adaptive renderer calculates bubble width
+separately for each width run using only the
+rows in that run.
|
|Consequently, if an older/discarded portion
+of a user message contains a long row while
+the visible full-width tail contains only
+short hard lines, the visible tail remains
+unnecessarily wide. A proper full-width
+suffix composition from fullStartIndex to
+endLineIndex would produce the same
+canonical row count while sizing the bubble
+from the visible full-width run.
|
|This does not lose text—the new continuity
+test correctly covers source ordering—but it
+is a visual behavior regression for user and
+queued-user bubbles spanning the narrow/full
+boundary. The current test uses a long
+single-line word flow, where the full tail
+naturally contains near-full rows and cannot
+expose this case.
|
|A targeted test should use:
|
|▎ text
|▎ <long hard line producing many rows>
|▎ short
|▎ tiny
|▎ x
|▎ ...
|
|with the final short hard lines occupying
+the full tail.
|
|────────────────────────────────────────
|
|### 4. Low — ANSI sourceRemainder is
+visually stable but not a true suffix of the
+canonical ANSI source
|
|Locations: render.ts:570-576,
+render.ts:685-714
|
|wrappedRemainders rebuilds source suffixes
+from wrapAnsiLine’s output chunks:
|
|▎ ts
|▎ remainder =
|▎   line[index] + join[index + 1] + remainde
+▎ r;
|
|Those chunks already contain synthetic
+resets and active-SGR prefixes inserted at
+wrap boundaries. When the selected probe row
+is recomposed, wrapping can add further
+empty active/reset pairs. For example, a
+suffix ending in styled text gains sequences
+equivalent to:
|
|▎ text
|▎ ... \\x1b[0m\\x1b[36m\\x1b[0m
|
|The visible text, startCol, joins, and
+styling remained equivalent in the cases I
+checked, so this is low severity. However,
+it violates the documented meaning of
+sourceRemainder as an ANSI-preserving
+remainder of the canonical line and makes
+suffix recomposition non-idempotent at the
+byte level.
|
|Prefer carrying the original ANSI source
+offset/state or a wrap-produced rest value
+rather than reconstructing source from
+rendered chunks.
|
|## Areas that appear correct
|
|- startCol accounting includes omitted
+soft-wrap joins correctly.
|- Continuation indentation reserves width
+before wrapping and is preserved when
+recomposing from a clipped assistant
+continuation.
|- The monotone canonicalLocal change is
