=== 1776571736084-5543fr#5 (gpt-5.4)
|Yes — you can do this as a Git alias.
|
|Git supports “shell aliases” when the alias
+value starts with !, so git sa can run a
+little shell script.
|
|Use this:
|
|▎ bash
|▎ git config --global alias.sa '!f() { for d
+▎  in */; do [ -d "$d/.git" ] || continue; p
+▎ rintf "== %s ==\\n" "$d"; git -C "$d" statu
+▎ s -sb; printf "\\n"; done; }; f'
|
|Then, from
+~/Workspace/Exocortex/external-tools/, run:
|
|▎ bash
|▎ git sa
|
|A few notes:
|
|- I used [ -d ... ] instead of [[ -d ...
+]] because Git shell aliases should be
+written portably, not assuming zsh.
|- git sa is nice if you think of this as a
+“git utility command”.
|- A zsh function is nicer if you want
+something easier to read/edit later.
|
|My recommendation:
|- if you want git sa, use a Git alias
|- if you want something more customizable,
+put it in ~/.zshrc as a function
|
|If you want the zsh version, I’d write it
+like this:
|
|▎ zsh
|▎ gitallstatus() {
|▎   for d in */; do
|▎     [[ -d "$d/.git" ]] || continue
|▎     echo "== $d =="
|▎     git -C "$d" status -sb
|▎     echo
|▎   done
|▎ }
|
|Then optionally add a shell alias too:
|
|▎ zsh
|▎ alias gsa=gitallstatus
|
|So:
|- git sa → Git alias approach
|- gsa or gitallstatus → zsh approach
|
|Honestly, for this exact use case, I
+slightly prefer the zsh function because
+it’s more readable and easier to tweak. But
+if you specifically want git sa, the Git
+alias works fine.
|
|If you want, I can give you a version of
+git sa that works on a passed path too,
+e.g. git sa
+~/Workspace/Exocortex/external-tools.
