+(unless buffer ends with \\n). But it doesn't
+prevent the cursor from sitting on a \\n
+character.
|
|Wait, let me re-read the issue more
+carefully. The user says pressing "a" can
+"go through line boundaries." Let me think
+about what charRight does vs what "a" does.
|
|charRight (used by "l") checks if
+(buffer[pos] === "\\n") return pos; — it
+won't cross a newline.
|
|But "a" does Math.min(cursor + 1,
+buffer.length) — it doesn't check if
+buffer[cursor] is \\n. So if the cursor is
+sitting on the last character of a line (not
+a \\n), cursor + 1 would be the \\n
+position, which is fine for insert mode
+(insert before the newline). But if the
+cursor IS on the \\n itself... hmm, can that
+happen?
|
|Actually wait — looking at clampNormal: it
+clamps to buffer.length - 1 unless the
+buffer ends with \\n. It doesn't prevent
+sitting on interior \\n characters. But the
+motions like charRight do prevent crossing
+\\n. And lineEnd ($) goes to the \\n position.
+So in normal mode, the cursor could be at a
+\\n position (e.g., after pressing $ on a
+non-last line).
|
|Actually, lineEndOf returns idx which is the
+position of the \\n. And $ calls lineEnd
+which calls lineEndOf. In normal mode with
+clampNormal, the \\n position wouldn't be
+clamped away (since it's not past
+buffer.length - 1 for interior newlines).
|
|Wait, but $ in real vim goes to the last
+character of the line, not to the \\n. Let me
+check — actually in this implementation,
+lineEnd returns the position of the \\n. In
+real vim, $ in normal mode goes to the last
+non-newline character. But this might be
+intentional here if the promptline is
+wrapping differently.
|
|OK, let me focus on the actual bug. When the
+cursor is at the \\n character (or
+equivalently at lineEndOf(buffer, cursor))
+and you press a, the cursor goes to cursor
++ 1, which would be the start of the next
+line. That's crossing a line boundary. In
+vim, a should move one position right from
+the cursor but stay on the same line.
|
|The fix: when computing the "after" cursor
+position, clamp it to not go past
+lineEndOf(buffer, cursor).
|
|▎ typescript
|▎ case "after": newCursor = Math.min(cursor 
+▎ + 1, lineEndOf(buffer, cursor)); break;
|
|This ensures that a never crosses a \\n
+boundary, just like l doesn't.
